 # Pre-Release Builds
 
-MeedyaDL ships through a **7-channel release pipeline** so testers can opt into different freshness/stability trade-offs. Stable users on `main` are unaffected by everything below — pre-release channels only surface in the in-app updater when you explicitly opt into them.
+MeedyaDL comes in four release channels, from least finished to most finished. Most people should stay on **Stable**. Pre-release channels only appear in the updater if you choose them.
 
-> **Heads-up.** This page replaces the legacy "meedyadl-v2 / pre-release.yml" docs. That branch and workflow were retired during the v1.x channelisation; everything below reflects the current pipeline as of v1.10.0-alpha.x.
+> The in-app Help page **Release Channels** is the main guide. This page covers the same ground.
 
-## Channel Overview
+## The Four Channels
 
-| Channel | Branch | Cadence | Driver workflow | Tag suffix |
-| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
-| **Stable** | `main` | Manual / release-please | `release-please.yml` → `release.yml` | none (`v1.10.0`) |
-| **Release Candidate** | `release-candidate` | Push-driven | `release-candidate-release.yml` → `release.yml` | `-rc.N` |
-| **Beta** | `beta` | Push-driven | `beta-release.yml` → `release.yml` | `-beta.N` |
-| **Alpha** | `alpha` | Push-driven | `alpha-release.yml` → `release.yml` | `-alpha.N` |
-| **Monthly** | `monthly` | Cron — `0 0 1 * *` (1st of month) | `monthly-release.yml` → `release.yml` | `-monthly.YYYYMMDD` |
-| **Weekly** | `weekly` | Cron — `0 0 * * 0` (Sundays) | `weekly-release.yml` → `release.yml` | `-weekly.YYYYMMDD` |
-| **Nightly** | `nightly` | Cron — `0 0 * * *` (daily) | `nightly-release.yml` → `release.yml` | `-nightly.YYYYMMDD` |
-
-**Channel promotion (ordering).** Internally the channels are partially ordered: `Nightly < Weekly < Monthly < Alpha < Beta < RC < Stable`. The in-app update checker treats this as **promotion**, not exact match — a user on Beta sees Beta, RC, and Stable updates; a user on Alpha additionally sees Alpha; etc. The backend updater refuses to install a tag that's *below* your selected channel, so a tampered manifest can't downgrade your stability.
-
-## Build Platforms
-
-All channels share the same 6-platform matrix as stable releases:
-
-### Tier 1 — Stable
-
-| Platform | Architecture | Installer |
+| Channel | Who it is for | Version ending |
 | --- | --- | --- |
-| macOS | Apple Silicon (ARM64) | `.dmg`, `.app` |
-| Windows | x64 | `.exe` (NSIS) |
-| Windows | ARM64 | `.exe` (NSIS) |
-| Linux | x64 | `.deb`, `.AppImage` |
-
-### Tier 2 — Experimental
-
-| Platform | Architecture | Installer |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Linux | ARM64 | `.deb` |
-| Linux | ARMv7 | `.deb` |
-
-## How Builds Get Triggered
-
-You almost never need to trigger a build by hand — pushing a `feat:`/`fix:` commit to a channel branch (or letting the cron fire) does the right thing.
-
-### Alpha / Beta / Release Candidate — push-driven
-
-The corresponding `*-release.yml` workflow watches each branch. On every push:
-
-1. Compute a monotonic counter across all existing tags of the channel (`vX.Y.Z-alpha.N`, etc.) — counters never reset across base-version bumps.
-2. Bump the version manifests (`package.json`, `tauri.conf.json`, `Cargo.toml`).
-3. Commit the version bump and push a new tag.
-4. The tag push fires `release.yml`, which builds the 6-platform matrix.
-5. The draft GitHub Release is **auto-published** for prereleases (stable drafts stay drafts pending maintainer review).
-
-A per-branch concurrency lock prevents two pushes from racing into the same counter.
-
-### Nightly / Weekly / Monthly — cron-driven
-
-Each cron workflow:
-
-1. Resets its channel branch (`nightly` / `weekly` / `monthly`) to `main`.
-2. Merges every `origin/feat/*` branch in turn (skips conflicting branches and opens a tracking issue for each conflict).
-3. Bumps version to `X.Y.Z-{channel}.YYYYMMDD`.
